{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_yx5aej5xciak3wazlli7hd6h3ucur5lszou6opuvqgy3bkvdgeha","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. All four phases complete.\n\n## What the program does\n\n`c_src/src/main.c` reads one integer with `scanf(\"%d\", &x)` (ignoring the return value), then hex-dumps the 4 raw bytes of that `int` in memory order, lowercase, plus `\\n`. Output is always exactly 9 bytes; stderr always empty.\n\n## Phase A — builds\n- C: `cd c_src/build && cmake .. && cmake --build .` → `c_src/build/driver`\n- Rust: `cd translation && cargo build --release` → `translation/target/release/driver`\n\nBoth built clean with no errors (verified again after a `rm -rf` clean rebuild). No compile errors needed fixing.\n\n## The one real mismatch I found\n\n**Rust exited 0 where the C program was killed by `SIGPIPE` (signal 13).**\n\nWith stdout on a pipe that has no reader, stdout and stderr matched *perfectly* — only the wait status differed. This is exactly the trap the task warns about; a stdout-only test would have passed.\n\nCause: the Rust stdlib sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` before `main` runs; C keeps the default. So C's exit-time flush died from the signal while Rust got `EPIPE`, discarded it via `let _ = ...`, and returned 0. Fixed by restoring `SIG_DFL` for `SIGPIPE` at the top of `main`, using a dependency-free `extern \"C\"` declaration of `signal(2)` (no new crates, so no network needed).\n\n## Phases B & C — 24 tests, ~5000 subprocess pairs\n\n`translation/tests/differential.rs` spawns both binaries and compares stdout, stderr, **and** exit status including death-by-signal. It never links the translation as a library, and it builds the C program itself if absent. It exercises both the debug and release Rust artifacts.\n\nBeyond the enumerated branches (empty input, whitespace-only, lone signs, non-digits, `INT`/`LONG` boundaries, leading zeros, 1–4097-digit runs, all 16 hex nibbles) I added exhaustive sweeps of length 0/1/2/3 inputs over the parser's alphabet, seeded random inputs, and every decimal near 2^0…2^64. C behaviours preserved rather than \"fixed\": garbage input silently prints `00000000` and exits 0; `%d` wraps via `long`→`int` (`4294967295` → `ffffffff`) but saturates only at the `long` boundary; `0x10` reads decimal `0`.\n\n## Verification worth flagging\n\nI mutation-tested the suite to prove it isn't passing vacuously: removing the SIGPIPE fix failed 1 test, clamping instead of truncating failed 5, uppercase hex failed 15.\n\nOne thing to note: while doing that, my backup file landed in a `$TMPDIR` that didn't persist between tool calls, so all three mutations accumulated in `src/main.rs`. I caught it, reverted each by hand, and confirmed with `grep` counts that zero mutation residue remains and all three original constructs are back. Final state: `cargo test` passes 24/24 in both debug and release, nothing `#[ignore]`d, and `c_src/` is untouched apart from the generated `build/` directory.\n\n`translation/ERRORS.md` records the mismatch, the preserved C quirks, the coverage table, and the mutation results."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3109,"cache_read_input_tokens":71603,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3109,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6b9f48b4-ce96-421c-8c95-9a646aa789f1","uuid":"7c447a35-8a5c-40f8-8690-8fd0e11f8b91","timestamp":"2026-08-28T15:49:12.731Z"}
